lly supported. DP measures counts per category, and with 74,000 categories, the privacy budget is consumed rapidly by composition."},"294":{"id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories-47","page_id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories#interaction-with-differential-privacy","content":"If you need DP protection on high-cardinality columns, declare a reduced subset of categories in your `column_schema`:"},"295":{"id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories-48","page_id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories#interaction-with-differential-privacy","content":"This is a tradeoff: you protect the most common categories but lose privacy guarantees on rare ones. Use this approach when privacy is a requirement but high-cardinality coverage is not critical."},"296":{"id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories-49","page_id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories#troubleshooting","content":"**Error: `code_set_corpus_too_many_values` or similar**"},"297":{"id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories-50","page_id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories#troubleshooting","content":"Your vocabulary exceeds 100,000 distinct values. Options:"},"298":{"id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories-51","page_id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories#troubleshooting","content":"Set `high_cardinality: false` (default) to reclassify as freetext"},"299":{"id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories-52","page_id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories#troubleshooting","content":"Use `allow_real_categories: false` to collapse rare values into `__other__`"},"300":{"id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories-53","page_id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories#troubleshooting","content":"Filter your source to a representative subset"},"301":{"id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories-54","page_id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories#troubleshooting","content":"**Output loses rare categories even with `high_cardinality: true`**"},"302":{"id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories-55","page_id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories#troubleshooting","content":"This happens with `allow_real_categories: false` (the default). Set `allow_real_categories: true`:"},"303":{"id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories-56","page_id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories#troubleshooting","content":"**Memory error during generation**"},"304":{"id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories-57","page_id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories#troubleshooting","content":"Your vocabulary is too large or the combined UTF-8 size exceeds 16 MiB. Try:"},"305":{"id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories-58","page_id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories#troubleshooting","content":"Reducing the source dataset size"},"306":{"id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories-59","page_id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories#troubleshooting","content":"Using a reduced category list"},"307":{"id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories-60","page_id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories#troubleshooting","content":"Setting `allow_real_categories: false` to collapse tail categories"},"308":{"id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories-61","page_id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories#see-also","content":"Work with healthcare code sets: code set masking and generation"},"309":{"id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories-62","page_id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories#see-also","content":"Generate synthetic data: full synthetic generation recipe"},"310":{"id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories-63","page_id":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/handle-high-cardinality-categories#see-also","content":"Differential privacy: privacy guarantees and limitations"},"311":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv","type":"page","content":"Mask a CSV","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Guides","Discover and mask sensitive data"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv"},"312":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv-0","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv","content":"Mask one CSV file with a mix of value-keyed and one-way strategies, and prove the keyed strategy is deterministic."},"313":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv-1","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv#prerequisites","content":"Prerequisites"},"314":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv-2","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv#1-write-a-pipeline-config","content":"1\\. Write a pipeline config"},"315":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv-3","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv#2-validate-before-running","content":"2\\. Validate before running"},"316":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv-4","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv#3-run-it","content":"3\\. Run it"},"317":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv-5","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv#4-add-a-keyed-strategy","content":"4\\. Add a keyed strategy"},"318":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv-6","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv#next","content":"Next"},"319":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv-7","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv","content":"This recipe masks a single CSV: a synthetic replacement for `email`, a\none-way redaction for `phone`, and a deterministic keyed hash for\n`customer_id` that stays stable across runs. It was run end to end against\n`decoy` CLI 0.1.0 (engine 0.4.0); every command block below is real output."},"320":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv-8","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv#prerequisites","content":"The `decoy` CLI installed (see install)."},"321":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv-9","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv#prerequisites","content":"A CSV with a header row. This recipe uses a `customers.csv` with columns\n`customer_id, first_name, last_name, email, phone, city, state, account_tier`."},"322":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv-10","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv#1-write-a-pipeline-config","content":"`pipeline.yaml`:"},"323":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv-11","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv#1-write-a-pipeline-config","content":"`email` gets a synthetic replacement, `phone` is redacted outright, and\n`account_tier` passes through unchanged because it carries no PII."},"324":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv-12","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv#2-validate-before-running","content":"`validate config` checks the YAML's schema and well-formedness (unknown\nprovider, bad strategy name, malformed YAML) without touching the source\nfile. It does not run every check `run` does: a strategy that needs a\n`namespace` (like `hash`) but doesn't have one still passes `validate\nconfig` and only surfaces when you `decoy run` it. Catch a typo'd provider\nhere; a missing namespace still needs a real run to catch."},"325":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv-13","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv#3-run-it","content":"`email` is a synthetic-but-realistic address, `phone` is the literal string\n`REDACTED`, and every other column is untouched."},"326":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv-14","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv#4-add-a-keyed-strategy","content":"`faker` and `redact` above need no secret: they are drawn from a seed alone.\nA **keyed** strategy (`hash`, `fpe`, `date_shift`, and a few others) instead\nderives its output from a mask secret, so the mapping is reproducible across\nruns and machines without the source values ever appearing in the config.\nAdd one to mask `customer_id` as an opaque, join-stable token:"},"327":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv-15","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv#4-add-a-keyed-strategy","content":"`hash` needs a `namespace` (it scopes the derivation) and a mask secret. Pass\nthe secret as `env:VAR_NAME` so the literal value never lands in the YAML or\nthe CLI's own argv. The 64-hex-char value below is a throwaway example;\ngenerate your own with something like `openssl rand -hex 32` and keep it out\nof version control:"},"328":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv-16","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv#4-add-a-keyed-strategy","content":"Running it a second time against the same secret and config produces a\nbyte-identical file. Save a copy of the first run's output, then run again\nand diff:"},"329":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv-17","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv#4-add-a-keyed-strategy","content":"That determinism is what makes `hash` and the other keyed strategies safe\nfor join-preserving masks: the same source value under the same secret and\nnamespace always lands on the same masked value, in this run and the next\none. See keys and the vault for how the\nsecret and the seed relate, and the full strategy catalog on the\nCLI reference."},"330":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv-18","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv#next","content":"Masking more than one table with a shared key: see\nmask a folder with foreign keys."},"331":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv-19","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv#next","content":"Recovering a `vault: true` or `fpe` column later: `decoy unmask` (see the\nCLI reference)."},"332":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv-20","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-csv#next","content":"Wiring this into a pipeline that gates on the result: see\nuse Decoy in CI."},"333":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys","type":"page","content":"Mask a folder with foreign keys","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Guides","Discover and mask sensitive data"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys"},"334":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys-0","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys","content":"Mask two related tables in one run so the join between them survives."},"335":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys-1","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys#prerequisites","content":"Prerequisites"},"336":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys-2","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys#1-declare-the-relationship","content":"1\\. Declare the relationship"},"337":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys-3","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys#2-validate-and-run","content":"2\\. Validate and run"},"338":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys-4","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys#3-confirm-the-join-actually-survived","content":"3\\. Confirm the join actually survived"},"339":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys-5","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys#next","content":"Next"},"340":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys-6","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys","content":"When a parent table and a child table share a key, masking them separately\nbreaks the join: `customers.customer_id` and `orders.customer_id` would each\nget remapped independently, and no order would point at the right customer\nanymore. This recipe masks both tables in one run and keeps the join intact.\nRun against `decoy` CLI 0.1.0; every command block is real output."},"341":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys-7","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys#prerequisites","content":"A `customers.csv` (`customer_id, first_name, last_name, email, phone, city,\nstate, account_tier`) and an `orders.csv` (`order_id, customer_id,\norder_date, total_amount, status`) with `orders.customer_id` referencing\n`customers.customer_id`."},"342":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys-8","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys#1-declare-the-relationship","content":"`pipeline.yaml`:"},"343":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys-9","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys#1-declare-the-relationship","content":"Three things bind the two tables together:"},"344":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys-10","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys#1-declare-the-relationship","content":"The `customer_id` column on **both** tables declares the same `namespace`\n(`customer_identity`)."},"345":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys-11","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys#1-declare-the-relationship","content":"The `relationships` block names the parent, the child, and repeats that\nnamespace."},"346":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys-12","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys#1-declare-the-relationship","content":"`orphan_policy` says what to do with a child row whose key has no matching\nparent. `preserve` leaves the orphan's key as-is; the other options are\n`remap`, `warn`, and `fail`. See the CLI reference `run` entry\nfor the full contract."},"347":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys-13","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys#1-declare-the-relationship","content":"You do not order the tables yourself: the engine builds an ordering from the\n`relationships` block so the parent is masked before its children."},"348":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys-14","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys#3-confirm-the-join-actually-survived","content":"The `customer_id` values look nothing alike between the two head samples\nabove because different source customers appear on those first rows in each\nfile. What matters is whether the SAME source `customer_id` maps to the SAME\nmasked value on both sides. Checking every row against the source data for\nthis run:"},"349":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys-15","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys#3-confirm-the-join-actually-survived","content":"All 50 orders resolved to the same masked `customer_id` their parent\ncustomer got. This holds for composite keys, self-referencing tables, and\nmulti-hop chains too; the mechanism is the same shared `namespace`."},"350":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys-16","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys#next","content":"What `orphan_policy` actually does to an unmatched child row, and how a\nrow-level masking error cascades across a relationship: see the\nCLI reference `run` and `validate` entries."},"351":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys-17","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys#next","content":"Cutting a small, referentially-intact slice instead of masking the whole\nfolder is an engine-core capability without a CLI verb yet; not covered\nhere."},"352":{"id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys-18","page_id":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/mask-a-folder-with-foreign-keys#next","content":"Gating a folder mask like this one in a pipeline: see\nuse Decoy in CI."},"353":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","type":"page","content":"Redact text with NER detection","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Guides","Handle specialized data"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner"},"354":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-0","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","content":"Activate named-entity recognition to detect and redact person names and locations in free text."},"355":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-1","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#what-text_redact-does","content":"What text_redact does"},"356":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-2","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#prerequisites","content":"Prerequisites"},"357":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-3","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#configuration","content":"Configuration"},"358":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-4","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#options","content":"Options"},"359":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-5","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#example-clinical-notes","content":"Example: Clinical notes"},"360":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-6","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#redact-vs-replace","content":"Redact vs. replace"},"361":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-7","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#entity-types-detected","content":"Entity types detected"},"362":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-8","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#caveats","content":"Caveats"},"363":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-9","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#determinism","content":"Determinism"},"364":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-10","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#performance","content":"Performance"},"365":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-11","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#troubleshooting","content":"Troubleshooting"},"366":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-12","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#examples","content":"Examples"},"367":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-13","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#example-1-admitdischarge-notes","content":"Example 1: Admit/discharge notes"},"368":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-14","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#example-2-replace-with-generated-values","content":"Example 2: Replace with generated values"},"369":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-15","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#see-also","content":"See also"},"370":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-16","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","content":"The `text_redact` strategy can use spaCy's named-entity recognition (NER) to automatically detect person names and location references in free text columns, then redact them. This page covers setup and usage."},"371":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-17","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#what-text_redact-does","content":"`text_redact` replaces sensitive text spans with a placeholder:"},"372":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-18","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#what-text_redact-does","content":"**Input**: \"Patient John Smith from Boston was admitted to Mass General\""},"373":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-19","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#what-text_redact-does","content":"**Output**: \"Patient \\[NAME] from \\[LOCATION] was admitted to Mass General\""},"374":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-20","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#what-text_redact-does","content":"NER detection is optional and off by default. Without it, `text_redact` uses pattern matching (phone numbers, email addresses, social security numbers). With NER enabled, it also detects person names and locations using the spaCy English model."},"375":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-21","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#prerequisites","content":"Install the NER extra:"},"376":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-22","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#prerequisites","content":"This pulls in spaCy and downloads the `en_core_web_sm` model. The model is \\~50 MB and downloaded once on first use."},"377":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-23","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#prerequisites","content":"If you get a warning about missing spacy model on the first run, run:"},"378":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-24","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#configuration","content":"Enable NER in your pipeline YAML:"},"379":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-25","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#options","content":"**`ner: true`**: Enable NER detection (requires `[ner]` extra). Detects PERSON and GPE (geopolitical entity, i.e., locations)."},"380":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-26","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#options","content":"**`redact_placeholder`** (optional): String to replace sensitive spans. Default is `[PII]`. Use `[REDACTED]`, `[NAME]`, `[LOCATION]`, or any custom string."},"381":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-27","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#options","content":"**`redact_replacement`** (alternative to placeholder): Replace with a synthetic value (e.g., a generated name) instead of a fixed placeholder. See below."},"382":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-28","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#example-clinical-notes","content":"Input CSV:"},"383":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-29","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#example-clinical-notes","content":"Configuration:"},"384":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-30","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#example-clinical-notes","content":"Output:"},"385":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-31","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#redact-vs-replace","content":"By default, `text_redact` with NER replaces spans with a placeholder like `[NAME]`. Alternatively, replace with a generated value:"},"386":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-32","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#redact-vs-replace","content":"This replaces person names with generated first names, and locations with random city names (if using a location provider)."},"387":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-33","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#entity-types-detected","content":"With NER enabled, Decoy detects and redacts:"},"388":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-34","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#entity-types-detected","content":"Entity"},"389":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-35","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#entity-types-detected","content":"Example"},"390":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-36","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#entity-types-detected","content":"Redacted"},"391":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-37","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#entity-types-detected","content":"PERSON"},"392":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-38","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#entity-types-detected","content":"John Smith, Dr. Sarah Johnson"},"393":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-39","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#entity-types-detected","content":"\\[NAME] or generated name"},"394":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-40","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#entity-types-detected","content":"GPE (location)"},"395":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-41","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#entity-types-detected","content":"Boston, Massachusetts, USA"},"396":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-42","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#entity-types-detected","content":"\\[LOCATION] or generated city"},"397":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-43","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#entity-types-detected","content":"PATTERN (heuristic)"},"398":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-44","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#entity-types-detected","content":"555-0123 (phone)"},"399":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-45","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#entity-types-detected","content":"\\[PII]"},"400":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-46","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#entity-types-detected","content":"PATTERN (heuristic)"},"401":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-47","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#entity-types-detected","content":"john\\@example.com (email)"},"402":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-48","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#entity-types-detected","content":"\\[PII]"},"403":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-49","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#entity-types-detected","content":"PATTERN (heuristic)"},"404":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-50","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#entity-types-detected","content":"123-45-6789 (SSN)"},"405":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-51","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#entity-types-detected","content":"\\[PII]"},"406":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-52","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#entity-types-detected","content":"Pattern-based detectors always run, regardless of the NER setting."},"407":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-53","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#caveats","content":"NER is probabilistic. The spaCy model is trained on news and web text, so it may:"},"408":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-54","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#caveats","content":"**Miss identifiers** (false negative): A person name written in lowercase or a specialized location not in the training data may be missed."},"409":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-55","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#caveats","content":"**Over-redact** (false positive): A word that looks like a name but is not (e.g., \"Hope\" as a verb, \"Apple\" as a fruit) may be flagged."},"410":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-56","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#caveats","content":"For healthcare text, clinical abbreviations and specialized terminology may confuse the model. Always review a sample of redacted output to check for missed or over-redacted spans."},"411":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-57","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#caveats","content":"**Best practice**: Run `text_redact` with NER as a first pass to catch common person names and places, then review the output manually or with a second detection pass."},"412":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-58","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#determinism","content":"With a fixed `global_settings.seed`, redaction is deterministic. Running the same pipeline twice produces identical output."},"413":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-59","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#determinism","content":"If `redact_replacement` is used, the generated replacement (e.g., a synthetic first name) is seeded and reproducible."},"414":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-60","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#performance","content":"Enabling NER adds overhead: the spaCy model must load and process each text value. For large text columns (thousands of rows), this can add 1-3 seconds per million characters, depending on text length and hardware."},"415":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-61","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#performance","content":"For production pipelines on large datasets, consider:"},"416":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-62","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#performance","content":"Running NER on a sample and spot-checking results"},"417":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-63","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#performance","content":"Masking common known identifiers with patterns only (NER disabled), then reviewing"},"418":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-64","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#performance","content":"Processing in chunks or batch mode"},"419":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-65","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#troubleshooting","content":"**Error: `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'spacy'`**"},"420":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-66","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#troubleshooting","content":"Install the NER extra:"},"421":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-67","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#troubleshooting","content":"**Error: `OSError: [E050] Can't find model 'en_core_web_sm'`**"},"422":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-68","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#troubleshooting","content":"Download the spaCy model:"},"423":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-69","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#troubleshooting","content":"**Output has too many false positives (over-redaction)**"},"424":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-70","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#troubleshooting","content":"Disable NER for this column:"},"425":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-71","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#troubleshooting","content":"Or review and manually adjust the redaction placeholder."},"426":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-72","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#troubleshooting","content":"**Output has too many false negatives (missed identifiers)**"},"427":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-73","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#troubleshooting","content":"This is common with domain-specific or non-standard text. Use a placeholder pass with NER, then add a secondary heuristic pass or manual review. Decoy's NER is intended as a first-line detection aid, not as a guarantee."},"428":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-74","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#example-1-admitdischarge-notes","content":"Input: \"Patient Maria Lopez was discharged home. Follow-up with Dr. Robert Chen at Mayo Clinic.\""},"429":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-75","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#example-1-admitdischarge-notes","content":"Output: \"Patient \\[REDACTED] was discharged home. Follow-up with Dr. \\[REDACTED] at \\[REDACTED].\""},"430":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-76","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#example-2-replace-with-generated-values","content":"Input: \"Patient John Doe from Chicago called to report symptoms.\""},"431":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-77","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#example-2-replace-with-generated-values","content":"Output: \"Patient Michael from Nashville called to report symptoms.\""},"432":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-78","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#see-also","content":"Mask a CSV: basic masking recipe"},"433":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-79","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#see-also","content":"Reference: strategies: full strategy catalog"},"434":{"id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner-80","page_id":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/redact-text-with-ner#see-also","content":"Security, privacy, and compliance limits: honest boundaries of NER detection"},"435":{"id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii","page_id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii","type":"page","content":"Scan for PII","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Guides","Discover and mask sensitive data"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii"},"436":{"id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii-0","page_id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii","content":"Profile a dataset with STORM, review what it found, and turn the findings into an explicit masking pipeline."},"437":{"id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii-1","page_id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii#1-scan-the-file","content":"1\\. Scan the file"},"438":{"id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii-2","page_id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii#2-review-the-findings","content":"2\\. Review the findings"},"439":{"id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii-3","page_id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii#3-turn-findings-into-a-pipeline","content":"3\\. Turn findings into a pipeline"},"440":{"id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii-4","page_id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii#4-check-the-result-caught-everything-you-meant-to-mask","content":"4\\. Check the result caught everything you meant to mask"},"441":{"id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii-5","page_id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii#next","content":"Next"},"442":{"id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii-6","page_id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii","content":"STORM profiles a dataset before you write a masking pipeline: what fields\nlook like PII, which columns form a quasi-identifier group together, and a\nre-identification risk score. It never masks anything and never applies a\npipeline on your behalf; it surfaces candidates for you to review. Run\nagainst `decoy` CLI 0.1.0; every command block below is real output."},"443":{"id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii-7","page_id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii","content":"STORM's detectors are heuristics. A finding is a candidate, not a verdict:\nyou read it, decide which mask strategy fits, and write that decision into\nthe YAML as an explicit `strategy:` entry. Nothing is masked until you do."},"444":{"id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii-8","page_id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii#1-scan-the-file","content":"`--rows N --strategy random` caps and samples large files instead of\nscanning everything; `--json` prints the full profile to stdout for piping\ninto other tooling."},"445":{"id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii-9","page_id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii#1-scan-the-file","content":"`decoy storm scan` is the old name for this command. It still works today\n(it prints a migration warning on stderr and runs `analyze` underneath) but\nis deprecated. Use `decoy storm analyze`."},"446":{"id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii-10","page_id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii#2-review-the-findings","content":"Drill into one field for its top values and detector detail:"},"447":{"id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii-11","page_id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii#2-review-the-findings","content":"`--pii high --quasi` on `storm fields` narrows the list to fields that are\nboth high-PII and part of a quasi-identifier group, and `--json` pipes just\nthe matching field names to something like `jq`."},"448":{"id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii-12","page_id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii#3-turn-findings-into-a-pipeline","content":"Passing the source file straight to `decoy init` scaffolds a pipeline from\nthe same STORM pass, with a `# REVIEW:` comment above every column it made a\ncall on:"},"449":{"id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii-13","page_id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii#3-turn-findings-into-a-pipeline","content":"Every scaffolded column carries a `REVIEW` note explaining why STORM picked\nthat strategy, not a silent guess. Read them, change any strategy that is\nwrong for your data, delete the comments, then validate and run:"},"450":{"id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii-14","page_id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii#4-check-the-result-caught-everything-you-meant-to-mask","content":"`decoy storm integrity` runs the same post-mask checks the platform runs\nwhen `run_storm: true` is set: residual PII, foreign-key preservation, and\npolicy validation against the config. Point it at the masked output and its\npre-mask source:"},"451":{"id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii-15","page_id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii#4-check-the-result-caught-everything-you-meant-to-mask","content":"Two `fail` findings, exit code `4`: the scaffolded strategies above\n(`truncate` on `customer_id`, `redact` on names) still left something the\ndetector considers PII. That is the point of running this check, not a bug\nin the recipe; the report names the exact column and detector so you know\nwhich `# REVIEW:` line to revisit. It exits 0 when nothing is flagged and\n`EXIT_FINDINGS` (4) whenever a column still carries real values through. See\nuse Decoy in CI for wiring that exit code\ninto a build gate, and STORM for why detection and\nmasking stay two separate, inspectable steps."},"452":{"id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii-16","page_id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii#next","content":"The full detector and exit-code contract: CLI reference\n`storm` entry."},"453":{"id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii-17","page_id":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/scan-for-pii#next","content":"A Disguise template is a starting point built from common findings for a\ngiven regulation; see disguises."},"454":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","type":"page","content":"Subset a dataset","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Guides","Build safer test datasets"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset"},"455":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-0","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","content":"Pull a small, referentially intact slice of related tables."},"456":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-1","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#the-subset-block","content":"The subset block"},"457":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-2","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#seed-modes","content":"Seed modes"},"458":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-3","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#settings","content":"Settings"},"459":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-4","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","content":"Seeds pick starting rows in a root table. Decoy follows the foreign keys you\ndeclared and pulls only the related rows, under a row budget, so the slice still\njoins. Subsetting reads Parquet and runs before masking."},"460":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-5","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#the-subset-block","content":"Add a `subset:` block to a pipeline that already declares its `relationships:`.\nThis keeps EU customers and just their orders:"},"461":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-6","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#the-subset-block","content":"Run it:"},"462":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-7","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#seed-modes","content":"Every seed picks rows one of three ways:"},"463":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-8","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#seed-modes","content":"Predicate `op` values: `eq`, `ne`, `lt`, `le`, `gt`, `ge`, `in`, `is_null`, `is_not_null`."},"464":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-9","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#settings","content":"Option"},"465":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-10","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#settings","content":"Type"},"466":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-11","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#settings","content":"Default"},"467":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-12","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#settings","content":"Description"},"468":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-13","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#settings","content":"`seeds`"},"469":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-14","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#settings","content":"list"},"470":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-15","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#settings","content":"required, min 1"},"471":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-16","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#settings","content":"Starting rows; each is a filter, sample, or keys seed"},"472":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-17","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#settings","content":"`budget.max_total_rows`"},"473":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-18","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#settings","content":"integer"},"474":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-19","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#settings","content":"none"},"475":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-20","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#settings","content":"Cap on total rows across all tables"},"476":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-21","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#settings","content":"`budget.max_table_seed_multiple`"},"477":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-22","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#settings","content":"number"},"478":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-23","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#settings","content":"none"},"479":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-24","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#settings","content":"Cap each table at this multiple of its seed count"},"480":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-25","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#settings","content":"`edge_directions`"},"481":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-26","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#settings","content":"map"},"482":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-27","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#settings","content":"both"},"483":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-28","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#settings","content":"Per-edge `both`, `downward`, `upward`, or `none`"},"484":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-29","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#settings","content":"`allow_dangling`"},"485":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-30","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#settings","content":"boolean"},"486":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-31","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#settings","content":"`false`"},"487":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-32","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#settings","content":"Allow child rows whose parent was not pulled"},"488":{"id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset-33","page_id":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/subset-a-dataset#settings","content":"The sources for every subsetted or related table must be `format: parquet`\n(subsetting does not run on CSV). For the full pipeline shape (sources, tables,\ntargets), see Quickstart."},"489":{"id":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data","page_id":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data","type":"page","content":"Unmask data","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Guides","Operationalize"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data"},"490":{"id":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data-0","page_id":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data","content":"Recover original values from a masked file."},"491":{"id":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data-1","page_id":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data#reverse-fpe-columns","content":"Reverse fpe columns"},"492":{"id":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data-2","page_id":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data#reverse-vaulted-columns","content":"Reverse vaulted columns"},"493":{"id":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data-3","page_id":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data#what-does-not-reverse","content":"What does not reverse"},"494":{"id":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data-4","page_id":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data","content":"Two kinds of column can be reversed: `fpe` columns (format-preserving encryption\nis a keyed bijection, so they reverse from the config's seed and namespace alone)\nand columns you marked `vault: true` (reversed with the vault file the mask run\nwrote). Every other strategy is one-way and passes through unchanged."},"495":{"id":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data-5","page_id":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data#reverse-fpe-columns","content":"`fpe` columns need only the config the mask run used:"},"496":{"id":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data-6","page_id":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data#reverse-fpe-columns","content":"This writes `masked.unmasked.csv`. Use `--output` to choose the path, and\n`--table` when the config masks more than one table."},"497":{"id":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data-7","page_id":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data#reverse-vaulted-columns","content":"To reverse a one-way column, mark it `vault: true` and write a vault at mask time:"},"498":{"id":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data-8","page_id":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data#reverse-vaulted-columns","content":"The vault is decrypted under the config's seed, so keep both the config and the\nvault file safe."},"499":{"id":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data-9","page_id":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/unmask-data#what-does-not-reverse","content":"`hash`, `redact`, `faker` (without `vault: true`), and the other one-way\nstrategies cannot be reversed. `decoy unmask` passes them through unchanged and\nmarks them `irreversible` in its report (add `--json` to see it)."},"500":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci","type":"page","content":"Use Decoy in CI","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Guides","Operationalize"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci"},"501":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci-0","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci","content":"Run decoy non-interactively, gate a build on its exit code, and keep the evidence artifact."},"502":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci-1","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci#1-fail-fast-validate-before-you-run","content":"1\\. Fail fast: validate before you run"},"503":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci-2","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci#2-run-non-interactively","content":"2\\. Run non-interactively"},"504":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci-3","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci#3-gate-on-the-exit-code-not-on-parsed-output","content":"3\\. Gate on the exit code, not on parsed output"},"505":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci-4","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci#next","content":"Next"},"506":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci-5","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci","content":"Every `decoy` command is one-shot and scriptable: no interactive prompts\noutside `decoy init`, a stable exit-code contract, and `--json`/`--quiet`\nflags for structured or silent output. This recipe covers the three things a\nCI job needs: fail fast on a bad config, run non-interactively, and gate on\nthe right exit code. Run against `decoy` CLI 0.1.0; every command block\nbelow is real output."},"507":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci-6","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci#1-fail-fast-validate-before-you-run","content":"`decoy validate config` and `decoy preflight` both catch problems before a\nrun starts, but they check different things. `validate config` is\nschema-only (unknown provider, bad strategy name, malformed YAML) - it does\nnot catch every runtime constraint, such as a `hash` column missing its\nrequired `namespace`, which only surfaces when you `decoy run` it;\n`preflight` adds local filesystem checks (does the source file exist, is it\nreadable, will a target overwrite something) on top of the same schema\ncheck. Neither touches platform secrets, RBAC, or network state; both are\nlocal-only."},"508":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci-7","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci#1-fail-fast-validate-before-you-run","content":"For a step that needs structured output instead of a human-readable line:"},"509":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci-8","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci#1-fail-fast-validate-before-you-run","content":"A broken config fails loud and specific instead of falling through to a\nconfusing error mid-run:"},"510":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci-9","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci#1-fail-fast-validate-before-you-run","content":"Loop it over every config in the repo so one bad file fails the build before\nany run starts:"},"511":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci-10","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci#1-fail-fast-validate-before-you-run","content":"Add `--fail-on-warning` on either command to also fail the build on an\nadvisory warning (for example, a target file that already exists and would\nbe silently overwritten)."},"512":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci-11","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci#2-run-non-interactively","content":"`decoy run` needs no prompts: pass `--json` for a structured result on\nstdout (progress goes to stderr, so the two never interleave), or\n`--quiet` for silence with the result carried entirely by the exit code.\n`--json` and `--quiet` are mutually exclusive; pick one:"},"513":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci-12","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci#2-run-non-interactively","content":"`--evidence-out` writes a local evidence manifest alongside the run: a\npipeline hash, input/output file fingerprints, and row counts/timings. It\nnever contains raw data values, which is what makes it safe to archive as a\nCI artifact:"},"514":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci-13","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci#2-run-non-interactively","content":"Upload `evidence.json` as a build artifact and a later `decoy evidence\nverify` run can confirm nothing drifted between the run and whatever\nconsumed its output."},"515":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci-14","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci#3-gate-on-the-exit-code-not-on-parsed-output","content":"`decoy validate distribution` and `decoy storm integrity` both use exit code\n`4` (`EXIT_FINDINGS`) for a clean run that found a real problem in the data,\ndistinct from `1` (bad input/usage) and `3` (the CLI itself broke). Treating\n4 the same as 1 or 3 in a CI script throws away that distinction."},"516":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci-15","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci#3-gate-on-the-exit-code-not-on-parsed-output","content":"`decoy validate distribution` recomputes fidelity between a pre-mask source\nand its output. In `report` mode (the default) it always exits 0, recording\nthe score without failing anything:"},"517":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci-16","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci#3-gate-on-the-exit-code-not-on-parsed-output","content":"Set `--mode fail` plus a threshold to make a real gate. Here `--min-score\n0.999` is stricter than the actual score (`0.997245`), so the check fails on\npurpose and exits `4`:"},"518":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci-17","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci#3-gate-on-the-exit-code-not-on-parsed-output","content":"`decoy storm integrity` uses the same convention: 0 clean, `4` on any\nfail-severity finding (residual PII, a broken FK, a policy mismatch), `1`\nfor missing/unreadable files, `3` for an unexpected crash. A build step that\nwants to treat \"the CLI ran fine but the data has a problem\" differently\nfrom \"the CLI blew up\" branches on the exit code directly:"},"519":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci-18","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci#3-gate-on-the-exit-code-not-on-parsed-output","content":"`decoy run` and `decoy validate config` use a simpler two-value contract (`0`\nsuccess, nonzero failure); a plain `decoy run pipeline.yaml` already gates\nthe job without any special-casing."},"520":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci-19","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci#next","content":"Why exit code `4` exists as its own value instead of folding into `1`:\nsee exit codes."},"521":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci-20","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci#next","content":"The full per-command exit-code table: CLI reference."},"522":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci-21","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-decoy-in-ci#next","content":"Turning STORM findings into the pipeline you are validating here: see\nscan for PII."},"523":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","type":"page","content":"Use differential privacy","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Guides","Build safer test datasets"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy"},"524":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-0","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","content":"Step-by-step recipe for generating differentially private synthetic data with epsilon/delta budgets."},"525":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-1","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#1-prepare-your-source-data","content":"1\\. Prepare your source data"},"526":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-2","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#2-declare-your-dp-configuration","content":"2\\. Declare your DP configuration"},"527":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-3","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#3-validate-and-run","content":"3\\. Validate and run"},"528":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-4","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#4-inspect-the-output","content":"4\\. Inspect the output"},"529":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-5","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#5-measure-utility-and-adjust","content":"5\\. Measure utility and adjust"},"530":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-6","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#6-privacy-budget-composition","content":"6\\. Privacy budget composition"},"531":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-7","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#7-constraints-and-limitations","content":"7\\. Constraints and limitations"},"532":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-8","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#next-steps","content":"Next steps"},"533":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-9","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","content":"This recipe generates synthetic data with a formal differential privacy guarantee. It walks through configuration, the privacy budget tradeoffs, and how to check the output."},"534":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-10","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#1-prepare-your-source-data","content":"Start with a CSV you want to generate from:"},"535":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-11","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#2-declare-your-dp-configuration","content":"Create a pipeline with `global_settings.dp` and a `column_schema` that declares:"},"536":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-12","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#2-declare-your-dp-configuration","content":"Which columns get DP protection"},"537":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-13","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#2-declare-your-dp-configuration","content":"Their type (numeric or categorical)"},"538":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-14","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#2-declare-your-dp-configuration","content":"Their domain (min/max for numeric, actual categories for categorical)"},"539":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-15","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#2-declare-your-dp-configuration","content":"**Key decisions:**"},"540":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-16","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#2-declare-your-dp-configuration","content":"**`epsilon: 1.0`**: Moderate privacy with reasonable fidelity. Start here for most use cases."},"541":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-17","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#2-declare-your-dp-configuration","content":"**`delta: 1e-6`**: Failure probability. Typical choice; smaller is stronger but may degrade fidelity."},"542":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-18","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#2-declare-your-dp-configuration","content":"**`column_schema`**: List only columns you want DP coverage for. Omit columns that are not sensitive or that you want to generate without DP (e.g., dates, free text)."},"543":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-19","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#2-declare-your-dp-configuration","content":"**`allow_real_categories: false`**: Prevents releasing actual category names. Set to true only if your category labels are public and non-sensitive."},"544":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-20","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#3-validate-and-run","content":"The output is 1,000 rows of synthetic data, generated from the DP snapshot."},"545":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-21","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#5-measure-utility-and-adjust","content":"Check whether the synthetic data is useful for your downstream task (testing, model training, analysis). Compare key statistics against the source:"},"546":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-22","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#5-measure-utility-and-adjust","content":"**Source statistics:**"},"547":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-23","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#5-measure-utility-and-adjust","content":"Age: mean=54, min=45, max=78"},"548":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-24","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#5-measure-utility-and-adjust","content":"Diagnosis codes: I10 (40%), E11 (35%), J44 (25%)"},"549":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-25","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#5-measure-utility-and-adjust","content":"**Synthetic statistics (with ε=1.0):**"},"550":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-26","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#5-measure-utility-and-adjust","content":"Age: mean=53, min=41, max=82"},"551":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-27","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#5-measure-utility-and-adjust","content":"Diagnosis codes: I10 (38%), E11 (37%), J44 (25%)"},"552":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-28","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#5-measure-utility-and-adjust","content":"If fidelity is too low, you can:"},"553":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-29","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#5-measure-utility-and-adjust","content":"**Increase epsilon** (weaker privacy, better fidelity): try 2.0 or 3.0"},"554":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-30","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#5-measure-utility-and-adjust","content":"**Increase delta** (higher failure probability, better fidelity): try 1e-5 (but accept higher risk)"},"555":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-31","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#5-measure-utility-and-adjust","content":"**Reduce the column count** (lower composition cost): drop non-critical columns"},"556":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-32","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#5-measure-utility-and-adjust","content":"If fidelity is acceptable, you now have synthetic data with a formal privacy guarantee."},"557":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-33","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#6-privacy-budget-composition","content":"Each column in your `column_schema` composes the privacy cost. If you declare k columns and set epsilon=1.0, the total spend is approximately:"},"558":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-34","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#6-privacy-budget-composition","content":"For 5 DP columns with epsilon=1.0, total epsilon ≈ 6.0. This is your absolute privacy budget across all released columns."},"559":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-35","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#6-privacy-budget-composition","content":"To stay within a total budget (e.g., 10):"},"560":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-36","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#6-privacy-budget-composition","content":"With 5 columns: use epsilon=10/6 ≈ 1.67 per column"},"561":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-37","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#6-privacy-budget-composition","content":"With 10 columns: use epsilon=10/11 ≈ 0.91 per column"},"562":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-38","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#6-privacy-budget-composition","content":"With 2 columns: use epsilon=10/3 ≈ 3.33 per column"},"563":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-39","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#7-constraints-and-limitations","content":"**Numeric and categorical only.** Dates, free text, and complex types are generated without DP protection. Declare them as non-DP types."},"564":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-40","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#7-constraints-and-limitations","content":"**No conditional generation.** `condition_on` is rejected under DP because joint distributions are not private. All columns are independent."},"565":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-41","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#7-constraints-and-limitations","content":"**Domain must be known upfront.** You declare age domain as \\[0, 120], diagnosis codes as a fixed list. The engine does not infer these from the data."},"566":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-42","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#7-constraints-and-limitations","content":"**Epsilon is enforced.** If you exceed the declared epsilon/delta budget, the job fails with an error."},"567":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-43","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#next-steps","content":"Differential privacy in Decoy: detailed explanation of the guarantee and its limits"},"568":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-44","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#next-steps","content":"Generate synthetic data: full recipe for non-DP synthetic generation"},"569":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy-45","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-to/use-differential-privacy#next-steps","content":"Security, privacy, and compliance limits: honest boundaries of all Decoy guarantees"},"570":{"id":"/docs/how-to/use-parquet","page_id":"/docs/how-to/use-parquet","type":"page","content":"Use Parquet files","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Guides","Discover and mask se