erence/capabilities-55","page_id":"/docs/reference/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/capabilities#execution-routes","content":"1 to 10 GB"},"718":{"id":"/docs/reference/capabilities-56","page_id":"/docs/reference/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/capabilities#execution-routes","content":"Out-of-core (DuckDB)"},"719":{"id":"/docs/reference/capabilities-57","page_id":"/docs/reference/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/capabilities#execution-routes","content":"Configurable cap, spills to disk"},"720":{"id":"/docs/reference/capabilities-58","page_id":"/docs/reference/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/capabilities#execution-routes","content":"10 GB and up"},"721":{"id":"/docs/reference/capabilities-59","page_id":"/docs/reference/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/capabilities#validation-and-safety","content":"Fail-closed: configuration errors are rejected at compile time, not run time."},"722":{"id":"/docs/reference/capabilities-60","page_id":"/docs/reference/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/capabilities#validation-and-safety","content":"Schema validation with descriptive errors."},"723":{"id":"/docs/reference/capabilities-61","page_id":"/docs/reference/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/capabilities#validation-and-safety","content":"Referential-integrity checks: FK cycles, dangling references, and invalid\nmasking topologies are caught before any data is written."},"724":{"id":"/docs/reference/capabilities-62","page_id":"/docs/reference/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/capabilities#validation-and-safety","content":"DP budget enforcement: declared epsilon/delta ceilings are checked."},"725":{"id":"/docs/reference/capabilities-63","page_id":"/docs/reference/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/capabilities#limits","content":"See Security, privacy, and compliance limits for what\nDecoy does not cover (masked output carries no DP guarantee; DP is per-column\nmarginals on generated data only; composite FKs are not sampled tuple-wise)."},"726":{"id":"/docs/reference/capabilities-64","page_id":"/docs/reference/capabilities","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/capabilities#version","content":"Decoy engine 0.7.0 (released 2026-10-01). The `decoy` CLI is source-available\nunder BUSL-1.1."},"727":{"id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes","page_id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes","type":"page","content":"Exit codes","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Reference"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/exit-codes"},"728":{"id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes-0","page_id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/exit-codes","content":"Why the exit-code contract is a design commitment, not an afterthought."},"729":{"id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes-1","page_id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/exit-codes#the-problem-a-flat-01-contract-has","content":"The problem a flat 0/1 contract has"},"730":{"id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes-2","page_id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/exit-codes#why-4-findings-is-its-own-code","content":"Why 4 (findings) is its own code"},"731":{"id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes-3","page_id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/exit-codes#why-the-values-are-stable","content":"Why the values are stable"},"732":{"id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes-4","page_id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/exit-codes#what-this-buys-you-in-ci","content":"What this buys you in CI"},"733":{"id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes-5","page_id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/exit-codes","content":"Every `decoy` subcommand returns one of a small set of stable integer exit\ncodes. The full table lives on the CLI reference; this page is\nabout why the contract is shaped the way it is."},"734":{"id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes-6","page_id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/exit-codes#the-problem-a-flat-01-contract-has","content":"A CLI that only ever returns 0 (success) or 1 (failure) forces every caller\nto re-parse output to figure out what actually went wrong. In a CI pipeline\nthat's expensive and brittle: you end up grepping stderr for magic strings\nto decide whether to retry, alert, or fail the build. The alternative is to\nput that information in the exit code itself, where a shell, a Makefile, or\na CI job can branch on it with a plain integer comparison."},"735":{"id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes-7","page_id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/exit-codes#why-4-findings-is-its-own-code","content":"The distinction that matters most for CI is between three different kinds of\n\"not a clean success\":"},"736":{"id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes-8","page_id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/exit-codes#why-4-findings-is-its-own-code","content":"**The caller's request was wrong.** A config that doesn't validate, a\npath that doesn't exist, an incompatible flag combination. The fix is in\nthe invocation, not the tool. This is exit 1."},"737":{"id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes-9","page_id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/exit-codes#why-4-findings-is-its-own-code","content":"**The tool itself broke.** The engine raised an unexpected error, an\noutput write failed. The fix is in the CLI or engine, not in what you\nasked for. This is exit 3."},"738":{"id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes-10","page_id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/exit-codes#why-4-findings-is-its-own-code","content":"**The tool ran fine, but the data has a problem.** `decoy storm\nintegrity` completed its check and found residual PII, or a foreign-key\npreservation check failed. Nothing about the invocation was wrong; the\ndata is what triggered the non-zero exit. This is exit 4."},"739":{"id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes-11","page_id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/exit-codes#why-4-findings-is-its-own-code","content":"Collapsing those three into one failure code would make a CI pipeline treat\na genuine data problem the same way it treats a typo in a config path or a\ntransient runtime crash. A pipeline that wants to fail the build on a real\nfinding, but retry or alert differently on a runtime error, needs the exit\ncode to carry that distinction. This is the same split semgrep and similar\nstatic-analysis tools use: a clean pass, a findings-nonzero exit, and a\ntool-error exit are three different things a script needs to react to\ndifferently."},"740":{"id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes-12","page_id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/exit-codes#why-4-findings-is-its-own-code","content":"Exit code `2` is not part of that three-way split; it is a narrower,\ndeprecated-invocation-and-warning-gate code. The legacy `forge` shim uses\nit to signal \"wrong binary, no-op.\" `validate config --fail-on-warning` and\n`preflight --fail-on-warning` also use it, deliberately, to let CI opt into\ntreating advisory warnings (like an output target that already exists) as\nbuild failures without conflating them with the schema/usage errors that\nown exit `1`."},"741":{"id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes-13","page_id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/exit-codes#why-the-values-are-stable","content":"The exit-code integers are a public contract: shell scripts, `Makefile`\ntargets, and CI job definitions branch on them directly. Decoy commits to\nnot moving an existing integer's meaning across releases; a new code can be\nadded for a genuinely new situation (exit 4 was added for the first\ndata-integrity command, ahead of any command that needed it), but 0 stays\nsuccess, 1 stays usage error, and so on. If that contract could shift\nsilently between versions, every downstream script that checks `$?` would\nneed to be re-audited on every upgrade."},"742":{"id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes-14","page_id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/exit-codes#what-this-buys-you-in-ci","content":"With a stable, specific exit code, a CI step can do this without parsing any\noutput:"},"743":{"id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes-15","page_id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/exit-codes#what-this-buys-you-in-ci","content":"That's the point of treating exit codes as a design commitment rather than\nwhatever `sys.exit()` happened to be called with at each call site: the\ncontract is what makes a `decoy` invocation safe to wire into an automated\npipeline without also shipping a wrapper that scrapes stdout to figure out\nwhat happened."},"744":{"id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes-16","page_id":"/docs/reference/exit-codes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/exit-codes#what-this-buys-you-in-ci","content":"See the CLI reference for the full table, and\nusing Decoy in CI for the complete recipe."},"745":{"id":"/docs/reference/limits","page_id":"/docs/reference/limits","type":"page","content":"Security, privacy, and compliance limits","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Reference"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/limits"},"746":{"id":"/docs/reference/limits-0","page_id":"/docs/reference/limits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/limits","content":"The honest boundary around what Decoy guarantees, so you don't rely on a promise it doesn't make."},"747":{"id":"/docs/reference/limits-1","page_id":"/docs/reference/limits","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/limits#it-provides-a-formal-privacy-guarantee-in-exactly-one-place","content":"It provides a formal privacy guarantee in exactly one place"},"748":{"id":"/docs/reference/limits-2","page_id":"/docs/reference/limits","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/limits#it-does-not-certify-legal-or-regulatory-compliance","content":"It does not certify legal or regulatory compliance"},"749":{"id":"/docs/reference/limits-3","page_id":"/docs/reference/limits","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/limits#it-does-not-guarantee-semantic-correctness-of-free-text","content":"It does not guarantee semantic correctness of free text"},"750":{"id":"/docs/reference/limits-4","page_id":"/docs/reference/limits","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/limits#it-does-not-validate-that-your-configuration-matches-your-intent","content":"It does not validate that your configuration matches your intent"},"751":{"id":"/docs/reference/limits-5","page_id":"/docs/reference/limits","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/limits#the-storm-classifier-does-not-de-identify-anything","content":"The STORM classifier does not de-identify anything"},"752":{"id":"/docs/reference/limits-6","page_id":"/docs/reference/limits","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/limits#format-preserving-encryption-has-known-edge-cases","content":"Format-preserving encryption has known edge cases"},"753":{"id":"/docs/reference/limits-7","page_id":"/docs/reference/limits","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/limits#correlation-checks-depend-on-the-strategy","content":"Correlation checks depend on the strategy"},"754":{"id":"/docs/reference/limits-8","page_id":"/docs/reference/limits","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/limits#what-it-does-do","content":"What it does do"},"755":{"id":"/docs/reference/limits-9","page_id":"/docs/reference/limits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/limits","content":"Decoy is a practical de-identification and synthetic-data tool. It applies\nrecognized transformation primitives (masking, hashing, format-preserving\nencryption, generalization, suppression, synthesis) and preserves structural\nproperties like foreign keys and determinism. This page is the honest\nboundary: the things Decoy does not prove, so you don't rely on a guarantee\nit doesn't make."},"756":{"id":"/docs/reference/limits-10","page_id":"/docs/reference/limits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/limits#it-provides-a-formal-privacy-guarantee-in-exactly-one-place","content":"Masked outputs carry no differential privacy, no epsilon, and no\nmathematical bound on re-identification risk. STORM's re-identification-risk\nscore is a heuristic diagnostic to help you assess a dataset; it is not a\nproof."},"757":{"id":"/docs/reference/limits-11","page_id":"/docs/reference/limits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/limits#it-provides-a-formal-privacy-guarantee-in-exactly-one-place","content":"The one formal mechanism is `decoy fit --epsilon`: a distribution snapshot's\ncounts are released with per-count noise under differential privacy, exact\nquantiles and means are removed, and min/max collapse to histogram-edge\nresolution. Read that scope narrowly:"},"758":{"id":"/docs/reference/limits-12","page_id":"/docs/reference/limits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/limits#it-provides-a-formal-privacy-guarantee-in-exactly-one-place","content":"The privacy budget is per column histogram. A snapshot of several columns\ncomposes sequentially, so releasing k columns spends roughly `(k + 1) *\nepsilon` of total budget."},"759":{"id":"/docs/reference/limits-13","page_id":"/docs/reference/limits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/limits#it-provides-a-formal-privacy-guarantee-in-exactly-one-place","content":"Bin edges and category labels stay data-dependent: the histogram range\ncomes from the real min and max, and categorical top values can carry real\ncategory strings when explicitly allowed. A fully data-independent release\nis a known follow-up, not current behavior."},"760":{"id":"/docs/reference/limits-14","page_id":"/docs/reference/limits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/limits#it-provides-a-formal-privacy-guarantee-in-exactly-one-place","content":"Joint (multi-column) distributions are not covered by `--epsilon` in the\ncurrent version; there is no composition accounting for releasing\nmarginals and a joint table under one budget yet."},"761":{"id":"/docs/reference/limits-15","page_id":"/docs/reference/limits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/limits#it-provides-a-formal-privacy-guarantee-in-exactly-one-place","content":"Nothing downstream of the snapshot inherits the guarantee. Generation\nsamples deterministically from the noisy artifact, and masking is entirely\noutside this mechanism."},"762":{"id":"/docs/reference/limits-16","page_id":"/docs/reference/limits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/limits#it-provides-a-formal-privacy-guarantee-in-exactly-one-place","content":"If your use case requires a formal privacy guarantee over the masked or\ngenerated data itself, Decoy alone does not supply it. Differential privacy\nin Decoy is scoped to the `--epsilon` snapshot path described above; it is\nnot a property of masked output in general."},"763":{"id":"/docs/reference/limits-17","page_id":"/docs/reference/limits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/limits#it-does-not-certify-legal-or-regulatory-compliance","content":"Decoy ships configuration bundles named after regulations, such as a HIPAA\nSafe Harbor Disguise that targets the 18 identifier categories a Safe Harbor\nreview commonly names. These are engineering aids that encode one reasonable\ninterpretation of an identifier set. They are not a compliance\ncertification, not legal advice, and not a determination that any given\noutput meets a regulation as applied to your data. Whether a masked dataset\nsatisfies HIPAA, GDPR, CCPA, or any other regime is a determination for you\nand your counsel, considering your data, your context, and the\nresidual-risk analysis the regulation requires. Running a bundle named\n`hipaa` does not by itself make a dataset HIPAA-compliant."},"764":{"id":"/docs/reference/limits-18","page_id":"/docs/reference/limits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/limits#it-does-not-guarantee-semantic-correctness-of-free-text","content":"Free-text redaction finds and replaces PII spans using pattern-and-hint\ndetection, optionally backed by named-entity recognition. Detection is\nbest-effort: it can miss an identifier the detectors don't recognize (a\nfalse negative), or replace a span that wasn't actually sensitive (a false\npositive). Decoy does not understand the meaning of free text and does not\nguarantee that every identifier in a notes column has been found, nor that\nthe surviving text stays semantically coherent after replacement. Treat\nfree-text output as reduced-risk, not proven-clean, and review it where the\nstakes warrant."},"765":{"id":"/docs/reference/limits-19","page_id":"/docs/reference/limits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/limits#it-does-not-validate-that-your-configuration-matches-your-intent","content":"Decoy validates a config against its schema and runs what the config says.\nIt does not know which columns in your data are actually sensitive. If a\nconfig leaves a sensitive column on `passthrough`, or masks the wrong\ncolumn, the run succeeds and the output leaks. Use STORM to find candidate\nPII and use post-mask integrity checks to look for residual identifiers, but\nthe mapping from your data's actual sensitivities to a correct config is\nyours to get right; see STORM for how that review\nstep is meant to work."},"766":{"id":"/docs/reference/limits-20","page_id":"/docs/reference/limits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/limits#the-storm-classifier-does-not-de-identify-anything","content":"STORM's column classifier detects which semantic type a column is likely to\ncontain (SSN, email, a diagnosis code, and so on). It does not mask, redact,\ntransform, or remove any values. A column classified as `ssn` still contains\nSSNs in the clear until a masking step in your pipeline explicitly\ntransforms it."},"767":{"id":"/docs/reference/limits-21","page_id":"/docs/reference/limits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/limits#the-storm-classifier-does-not-de-identify-anything","content":"The classifier is an advisory signal, not a ground truth. It can have false\nnegatives (PII columns it misses) and false positives (non-PII columns it\nflags). It was trained without any real PII entering its weights, but that\nis a training-data hygiene property, not a differential-privacy guarantee\nabout what the model outputs."},"768":{"id":"/docs/reference/limits-22","page_id":"/docs/reference/limits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/limits#format-preserving-encryption-has-known-edge-cases","content":"Format-preserving encryption (`fpe`) is designed to permute in-charset\ncharacters and pass separators through unchanged. Two edge cases are worth\nknowing:"},"769":{"id":"/docs/reference/limits-23","page_id":"/docs/reference/limits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/limits#format-preserving-encryption-has-known-edge-cases","content":"A value made entirely of characters outside the configured charset is\nrouted through a fallback so it isn't emitted verbatim. A value with a\n**mix** of in-charset and out-of-charset characters is not covered by that\nfallback: the in-charset characters are permuted, but the out-of-charset\ncharacters pass through in the clear, by the same \"separators pass\nthrough\" rule that makes FPE useful for values like formatted phone\nnumbers in the first place. If a column's real alphabet is wider than the\ncharset you configured (for example, a mostly-numeric ID that sometimes\ncarries letters), those letters can leak. Widen the charset to cover the\ncolumn's full alphabet, or use a strict orphan-value policy that rejects\nvalues outside the expected shape instead of passing them through."},"770":{"id":"/docs/reference/limits-24","page_id":"/docs/reference/limits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/limits#format-preserving-encryption-has-known-edge-cases","content":"The automated verbatim-leak check has limited signal on very low-entropy\ncolumns: distinguishing a real leak from a value that is legitimately\nalways the same (a checksum digit, for instance) gets harder the less\nvariety a column's source data has. It surfaces as an explicit skip\nrather than a silent pass, but it is still on you to widen the charset,\nor use a strict orphan-value policy, for columns with a mixed or unusual\nalphabet."},"771":{"id":"/docs/reference/limits-25","page_id":"/docs/reference/limits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/limits#correlation-checks-depend-on-the-strategy","content":"A value-changing mask (`fpe`, `hash`, `code_set`, and similar) relabels\nevery cell, so a naive comparison of masked and source distributions can\nshow near-zero similarity even when the underlying correlation structure was\npreserved perfectly, because the labels no longer overlap. A relabel-aware\nstatistic (comparing contingency counts rather than value labels) is needed\nto check correlation preservation across a bijective mask correctly, and\nthat is what Decoy's acceptance checks use for categorical column pairs.\nThat check does not extend to high-cardinality continuous columns, or to\nstrategies that merge distinct source values together (`bucketize`,\n`geo_generalize`, and similar), since those change the contingency structure\nitself rather than just relabeling it."},"772":{"id":"/docs/reference/limits-26","page_id":"/docs/reference/limits","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/limits#what-it-does-do","content":"To be clear about the other side: Decoy does give you deterministic,\nreproducible masking; foreign-key and join preservation across tables; a\ncatalog of standard de-identification transforms; PII detection and risk\nprofiling; and synthetic-data generation. Those features are real and\nexercised by the project's test suite. This page exists so the strength of\nthose features is not mistaken for guarantees Decoy does not make."},"773":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","type":"page","content":"Synthetic data providers","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Reference"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/providers"},"774":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers-0","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/providers","content":"The named value sources for the faker strategy and generation type."},"775":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers-1","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/providers#people","content":"People"},"776":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers-2","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/providers#addresses","content":"Addresses"},"777":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers-3","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/providers#composites","content":"Composites"},"778":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers-4","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/providers#identifiers-deterministic","content":"Identifiers (deterministic)"},"779":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers-5","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/providers#identifiers-faker","content":"Identifiers (faker)"},"780":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers-6","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/providers#generic","content":"Generic"},"781":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers-7","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/providers#usage","content":"Usage"},"782":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers-8","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/providers","content":"A provider is the value source behind the `faker` mask strategy and the `faker`\ngeneration type. Set it with `provider:` on a column. There are 34, across people,\naddresses, identifiers, and composites. The `decoy_native` providers are\ndeterministic and checksummed; the faker ones are random per run unless the column\nsets `deterministic: true`."},"783":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers-9","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/providers#people","content":"Provider"},"784":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers-10","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/providers#people","content":"Generates"},"785":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers-11","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/providers#people","content":"Backend"},"786":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers-12","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/providers#people","content":"`person_name`"},"787":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers-13","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/providers#people","content":"A full name"},"788":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers-14","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/providers#people","content":"faker"},"789":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers-15","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/providers#people","content":"`person_first_name`"},"790":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers-16","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/providers#people","content":"A first name"},"791":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers-17","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/providers#people","content":"faker"},"792":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers-18","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/providers#people","content":"`person_last_name`"},"793":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers-19","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/providers#people","content":"A last name"},"794":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers-20","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/providers#people","content":"faker"},"795":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers-21","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/providers#people","content":"`person_full_name`"},"796":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers-22","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/providers#people","content":"A full name"},"797":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers-23","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/providers#people","content":"faker"},"798":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers-24","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/providers#people","content":"`person_email`"},"799":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers-25","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/providers#people","content":"An email address"},"800":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers-26","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/providers#people","content":"faker"},"801":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers-27","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/providers#people","content":"`person_phone`"},"802":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers-28","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/providers#people","content":"A phone number"},"803":{"id":"/docs/reference/providers-29","page_id":"/docs/reference/providers","t