e connector importable for direct engine-SDK use; it\ndoes not add a pipeline YAML source or target type. Wiring `sftp` into\n`sources:` / `targets:` the way `s3` and `gcs` are wired is on the\nroadmap, not shipped."],[168,"That output is a JSON profile you can inspect, pipe to other tools, or use\ndirectly to decide which columns need which mask strategy."],[743,"That's the point of treating exit codes as a design commitment rather than\nwhatever `sys.exit()` happened to be called with at each call site: the\ncontract is what makes a `decoy` invocation safe to wire into an automated\npipeline without also shipping a wrapper that scrapes stdout to figure out\nwhat happened."],[344,"The `customer_id` column on **both** tables declares the same `namespace`\n(`customer_identity`)."],[348,"The `customer_id` values look nothing alike between the two head samples\nabove because different source customers appear on those first rows in each\nfile. What matters is whether the SAME source `customer_id` maps to the SAME\nmasked value on both sides. Checking every row against the source data for\nthis run:"],[320,"The `decoy` CLI installed (see install)."],[14,"The `decoy` CLI is source-available under the Business Source License 1.1\n(BUSL-1.1)."],[1003,"The `minimal` preset scaffolds the smallest sensible pipeline: name and email\nswapped for synthetic values, SSN redacted, everything else passed through.\nIt already points at `./input.csv` and `./output.csv`, which matches the file\nabove, so nothing needs editing for this walkthrough:"],[345,"The `relationships` block names the parent, the child, and repeats that\nnamespace."],[370,"The `text_redact` strategy can use spaCy's named-entity recognition (NER) to automatically detect person names and location references in free text columns, then redact them. This page covers setup and usage."],[2616,"The 23 mask strategies, grouped by what they do."],[2425,"The aggregate: `sum`, `mean`, `min`, `max`, or `count`"],[770,"The automated verbatim-leak check has limited signal on very low-entropy\ncolumns: distinguishing a real leak from a value that is legitimately\nalways the same (a checksum digit, for instance) gets harder the less\nvariety a column's source data has. It surfaces as an explicit skip\nrather than a silent pass, but it is still on you to widen the charset,\nor use a strict orphan-value policy, for columns with a mixed or unusual\nalphabet."],[57,"The budget `(epsilon, delta)` bounds the per-column marginal snapshot: the measured statistics are indistinguishable, up to that budget, between two runs on datasets that differ by one row. Decoy measures each column's marginal independently; it does not account for a joint guarantee over the whole synthetic table, and the generated rows are a deterministic sample from the noisy snapshot. See Security, privacy, and compliance limits for exactly what the generated output does and does not inherit."],[1026,"The catalog is backed by DuckDB at .decoy/catalog.duckdb. Entries are\nadded with `decoy catalog add`. This command is read-only."],[2503,"The character set to encrypt within"],[767,"The classifier is an advisory signal, not a ground truth. It can have false\nnegatives (PII columns it misses) and false positives (non-PII columns it\nflags). It was trained without any real PII entering its weights, but that\nis a training-data hygiene property, not a differential-privacy guarantee\nabout what the model outputs."],[990,"The CLI runs entirely locally: there is no server, no account, and no\ntelemetry phone-home. Input and output data stay on your machine unless a\npipeline's own `targets:` say otherwise."],[2421,"The column to aggregate"],[2659,"The columns masked jointly"],[2218,"The complete field-by-field pipeline schema, for editor validation."],[2924,"The connector class itself is real and unit-tested: password auth, or a\nPEM private key (RSA, Ed25519, ECDSA, DSA, auto-detected), strict host-key\nverification against `known_hosts